 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Strengths:** Reuses mature reconstruction pipelines and makes system-level engineering straightforward [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Prone to Janus ambiguity, over-smoothing, or texture oversaturation without additional regularizers [S1]. Inherits fundamental limitations of the multi-view generator, such as imperfect cross-view consistency leading to noisy results [S1].
-- **Performance:** Computational cost is high, requiring minutes to hours per prompt [S1].
-- **When to use:** When leveraging existing diffusion models and reconstruction pipelines is preferred over training end-to-end direct prediction models [S1].
-- **When not to use:** When strict cross-view consistency is critical and the multi-view generator cannot provide sufficient geometric constraints [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Significantly faster than iterative optimization-based reconstruction because the reconstruction is performed via a feed-forward network after the initial diffusion step [S2]. However, the total time is dominated by the multi-view diffusion generation, which can take minutes to hours per prompt [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced compared to dense voxel-based methods due to the sparse nature of Gaussians and the asymmetric U-Net design limiting output size [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Straightforward system-level engineering; reuses mature reconstruction pipelines; decouples generation from reconstruction [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Inherits fundamental limitations of the multi-view generator, including cross-view inconsistency and potential geometric artifacts [S1].
+
 ## Background
-Originating from the observation that direct generation is prone to artifacts, this method was popularized by models like LGM [S1, S2]. It builds on the idea that separating generation into synthesis and reconstruction allows for better reuse of existing technologies [S1]. LGM first exploited off-the-shelf models for multi-view generation and then trained a U-Net-based network for GS reconstruction [S2].
+This approach emerged as a strategy to mitigate the high computational costs and instability of direct 3D generation. By separating the problem into image synthesis and 3D reconstruction, it allows for the use of powerful, pre-trained 2D diffusion models [S1]. The Large Reconstruction Model (LGM) is a prominent example of this paradigm, utilizing off-the-shelf models for multi-view generation and training a U-Net for Gaussian reconstruction [S2].
